event: final
data: {"type": "thinking", "id": "t1", "content": "Open-rate drops have a small number of usual causes and they're worth ranking by likelihood before I go looking, because the diagnostic path is different for each and some are much more expensive to check.\n\nFirst: deliverability. If mail stopped reaching inboxes, the open rate falls even though nothing about the content changed. This is the most common cause and the cheapest to rule in or out — bounce logs, SPF/DKIM/DMARC status, and whether any major inbox provider started foldering us. It's also the most urgent if true, because sender reputation damage compounds across subsequent sends.\n\nSecond: list composition. If a batch of low-quality addresses entered the list, the denominator grows without the numerator following. This looks identical to a deliverability problem in the aggregate number but has a completely different fix, and the two are causally linked — a bad import produces hard bounces, which then damages reputation, which then hurts the good addresses too. So a list event can masquerade as a deliverability event while actually being both.\n\nThird: content and timing. Subject-line fatigue, send-time drift, a shift in the mix of campaign types. Real, but slow-moving — it rarely produces a sharp week-over-week drop, and the user described this as sudden.\n\nFourth: measurement. Apple Mail Privacy Protection and similar make open rates noisy in a way that can shift when audience composition changes. Worth holding in mind but not worth leading with.\n\nGiven \"last week\" specifically, I'd expect a discrete event rather than a trend. That points hard at one of the first two. Plan: scan bounce and deliverability logs across the last fourteen days so I have a before-and-after, look for a spike concentrated on a single send, and if I find one, check what entered the list immediately before it. If the bounces cluster on one send and that send included a segment the others didn't, that's the answer and everything else is noise.\n\nI should be careful not to report the aggregate open rate as the finding. The aggregate is the symptom. The user needs to know which send, which segment, and what to do before the next one goes out."}

